      "message": {
       "content": "# Critique of \"Effective Context Length\"\n\n## The metric as stated\n\n> *Effective context length = the longest context at which a model still answers a retrieval question correctly.*\n\nIt is a single scalar derived from a binary pass/fail on one (or a few) retrieval probes. That design has at least six structural problems.\n\n### 1. It is a threshold, not a curve\n\"Longest context at which it still answers correctly\" is a step function in disguise",
       "name": null,
       "reasoning": "The user is asking me to critique a proposed metric called \"effective context length\" and then propose a better definition that separates retrieval depth from reasoning depth. Let me think carefully about this.\n\nThe proposed metric: \"effective context length\" = the longest context at which a model still answers a retrieval question correctly.\n\nLet me critique this thoroughly:\n\n**Problems with the proposed metric:**\n\n1. **Binary threshold is fragile**: It defines a single number (the longest context where the model gets it right). This is a step function in disguise. A model that gets 99% correct at 10k tokens and 1% correct at 10.1k tokens has an \"effective context length\" of 10k, but a model that gets 50% correct at 10k and 49% at 10.1k has an \"effective context length\" of 10k too. The metric throws away the shape of the degradation curve.\n\n2. **Single-question / single-trial semantics**: \"Answers a retrieval question correctly\" \u2014 is this one question? One trial? What's the threshold? 100%? 50%? 90%? The definition is underspecified. If it's \"still answers correctly\" it implies 100%, which is absurdly strict and makes the metric extremely noisy.\n\n3. **Conflates retrieval with reasoning**: A retrieval question (\"What was the capital of X mentioned in the document?\") tests whether the model can find a fact in context. But many \"retrieval\" questions also require reasoning (multi-hop, aggregation, comparison). The metric doesn't separate \"can you find the needle\" from \"can you reason over what you found.\"\n\n4. **Position bias / lost-in-the-middle**: The metric as stated doesn't specify where the relevant information is placed. If you always put it at the beginning, you're not measuring context length at all \u2014 you",
